517
00:21:23.830 --> 00:21:27.250
On our flight, there's a man across the aisle with long, stringy hair.

518
00:21:27.510 --> 00:21:28.390
Like, crazy long.

519
00:21:28.430 --> 00:21:29.970
But he doesn't have much of it.

520
00:21:30.070 --> 00:21:31.270
Like, it's super thin.

521
00:21:31.670 --> 00:21:34.010
He has it pulled back into the saddest ponytail.

522
00:21:34.470 --> 00:21:38.070
I'm looking at the pen I'm riding with, and his ponytail is about the same width.

523
00:21:38.270 --> 00:21:38.950
Bless his heart.

524
00:21:40.570 --> 00:21:41.430
Oh, wow.

525
00:21:43.530 --> 00:21:45.350
I got a groan from Matt.

526
00:21:46.350 --> 00:21:51.210
Wednesday, April 1st, 2.59 p.m., flying from Denver to Seattle on United.

527
00:21:51.550 --> 00:21:53.290
Our college tours are going well.

528
00:21:53.390 --> 00:21:55.370
We had a great time with Josh's family in Colorado.

529
00:21:55.910 --> 00:21:59.270
We're waiting for our flight to Seattle to go tour the University of Washington.

530
00:21:59.270 --> 00:22:03.430
Josh is super excited because it's supposed to be overcast with a chance of rain there,

531
00:22:03.570 --> 00:22:04.730
his favorite kind of weather.

532
00:22:05.190 --> 00:22:06.010
Hold up.

533
00:22:06.370 --> 00:22:07.650
Oh, my God.

534
00:22:07.950 --> 00:22:13.210
Right now, they are paging a passenger over the intercom at the Denver airport.

535
00:22:13.450 --> 00:22:15.090
Y'all, this is amazing.

536
00:22:15.370 --> 00:22:18.910
They are saying, paging Idris Elby.

537
00:22:19.930 --> 00:22:21.770
Passenger, Idris Elby.

538
00:22:22.030 --> 00:22:23.810
I mean, come on.

539
00:22:23.810 --> 00:22:28.190
I need Idris Elby and Idris Elba to meet just once.

540
00:22:28.190 --> 00:22:28.990
Am I right?

541
00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:31.850
Now, on our flight.

542
00:22:32.010 --> 00:22:34.390
So, on United, you get a choice of three snack boxes.

543
00:22:34.550 --> 00:22:35.110
They are $10.

544
00:22:35.490 --> 00:22:36.270
Here are your options.

545
00:22:37.130 --> 00:22:42.330
The tapas box, hummus, pita chips, bruschetta dip, rosemary crackers, green olives, and almonds.

546
00:22:42.590 --> 00:22:47.970
The takeoff box, salami, garlic and herb crostini, gouda cheese spread, crackers, fig spread,

547
00:22:48.150 --> 00:22:49.650
almonds, hazelnut, wafer.

548
00:22:49.970 --> 00:22:55.510
The recline box, potato chips, beef and pork stick, fruit, gummy bears, Oreo cookies, almonds.

549
00:22:55.810 --> 00:22:56.770
Guess what I picked?

550
00:22:57.130 --> 00:22:57.490
Anyone?

551
00:22:58.190 --> 00:22:59.290
The hummus one again?

552
00:22:59.550 --> 00:23:00.970
I got the tapas box.

553
00:23:01.070 --> 00:23:01.850
It was delicious.

554
00:23:02.610 --> 00:23:03.930
Also, just a heads up.

555
00:23:04.030 --> 00:23:06.830
This United flight offered Sauvignon Blanc and not Chardonnay.

556
00:23:06.990 --> 00:23:07.530
Yas!

557
00:23:10.050 --> 00:23:10.610
Okay.

558
00:23:10.990 --> 00:23:11.830
Here we go.

559
00:23:12.050 --> 00:23:13.270
Last entry, everybody.

560
00:23:14.150 --> 00:23:18.410
Friday, April 3rd, 1135 AM, Seattle to San Francisco.

561
00:23:18.850 --> 00:23:20.030
Waiting for our flight.

562
00:23:20.210 --> 00:23:22.810
The man next to me is talking loudly on speakerphone.

563
00:23:23.010 --> 00:23:25.410
He's excitedly talking to someone named Claire.

564
00:23:25.650 --> 00:23:28.030
He keeps saying, oh, Claire, and then laughing.

565
00:23:28.290 --> 00:23:29.470
I shit you not.

566
00:23:29.750 --> 00:23:34.430
When he hangs up the phone, which I hope is soon, I want to say, so, Claire and you still

567
00:23:34.430 --> 00:23:35.270
a thing, huh?

568
00:23:40.350 --> 00:23:41.570
Anyway, where was I?

569
00:23:41.710 --> 00:23:42.610
Oh, Seattle was amazing.

570
00:23:42.770 --> 00:23:43.190
First of all.

571
00:23:43.230 --> 00:23:43.670
Wait, wait, wait.

572
00:23:43.970 --> 00:23:48.130
Is anyway, where was I something you just said or did you write that down?

573
00:23:48.450 --> 00:23:48.610
I wrote that.

574
00:23:49.150 --> 00:23:50.130
I thought so.

575
00:23:50.270 --> 00:23:51.910
Because I think I'm talking to someone.

576
00:23:52.170 --> 00:23:52.270
Okay.

577
00:23:53.050 --> 00:23:54.170
Anyway, where was I?

578
00:23:54.250 --> 00:23:54.890
I'm ridiculous.

579
00:23:55.170 --> 00:23:55.370
Amazing.

580
00:23:55.810 --> 00:23:56.930
Anyway, where was I?

581
00:23:57.110 --> 00:23:58.210
Oh, Seattle was amazing.

582
00:23:58.350 --> 00:24:02.130
First of all, it was cherry blossom season, and there were rows and rows of trees blooming.

583
00:24:02.290 --> 00:24:04.770
We walked under this pink canopy, just gorgeous.

584
00:24:05.110 --> 00:24:09.550
And hands down, our favorite thing we did as a family on this trip was a floating sauna.

585
00:24:09.910 --> 00:24:14.370
We stayed at the Woodmark Hotel, which I kept calling the Woodward and annoying it.

586
00:24:15.410 --> 00:24:17.050
It's right on Lake Washington.

587
00:24:17.050 --> 00:24:18.970
Thanks to Joel McHale for the recommendation.

588
00:24:18.970 --> 00:24:19.970
We loved it.

589
00:24:20.270 --> 00:24:20.450
Okay.

590
00:24:20.630 --> 00:24:24.710
Our first day there, we went for a walk and saw these people coming out of what looked

591
00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:26.250
like a little house on the water.

592
00:24:26.370 --> 00:24:30.670
And then they ran down this long pier and jumped into the lake and howled and laughed.

593
00:24:31.150 --> 00:24:33.290
Josh said, whatever that is, we got to do it.

594
00:24:33.730 --> 00:24:38.410
Turns out you can go into these little tiny floating saunas and do a cold plunge right off

595
00:24:38.410 --> 00:24:38.810
the dock.

596
00:24:38.990 --> 00:24:41.450
They have a ladder and everything to climb back out of the water.

597
00:24:41.630 --> 00:24:47.010
It was called Vaughn Sauna, and we did the, quote, social sauna, where you just book your

598
00:24:47.010 --> 00:24:48.410
seat and you get an hour.

599
00:24:48.610 --> 00:24:51.490
So our family was paired with a couple celebrating a birthday.

600
00:24:51.670 --> 00:24:55.330
But I guess if you want to get fancy, you can rent the whole thing out as a private sauna

601
00:24:55.330 --> 00:24:55.950
experience.

602
00:24:56.230 --> 00:24:57.730
But the couple we met was lovely.

603
00:24:57.930 --> 00:25:01.150
And so save your money and go for the cheaper social sauna experience.

604
00:25:02.230 --> 00:25:06.530
And then I wrote, and Jenna, I know I'll read this to you, but I thought about you the

605
00:25:06.530 --> 00:25:07.190
whole time.

606
00:25:07.350 --> 00:25:08.550
You would have loved this.

607
00:25:08.670 --> 00:25:12.170
I wasn't sure if I had the courage to do the plunge because I hate being cold.

608
00:25:12.390 --> 00:25:14.530
It's a thing anyone who knows me knows about me.

609
00:25:14.530 --> 00:25:18.290
My sister Janet once bought me a sweatshirt that said, I'm cold on it.

610
00:25:18.530 --> 00:25:19.730
But I did it.

611
00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:23.710
It was overcast and raining off and on and in the mid-50s outside.

612
00:25:23.910 --> 00:25:25.350
But we went to that little sauna.

613
00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:27.590
And by the time we came out of there, we were so hot.

614
00:25:27.670 --> 00:25:29.290
And we ran down the pier and we jumped in.

615
00:25:29.390 --> 00:25:30.710
The whole family did it.

616
00:25:31.110 --> 00:25:34.330
And I asked a guy who worked there what the temperature of Lake Washington was.

617
00:25:34.330 --> 00:25:36.310
He said probably around 46 degrees.

618
00:25:37.010 --> 00:25:37.410
OMG.

619
00:25:38.050 --> 00:25:38.450
Wow.

620
00:25:39.010 --> 00:25:43.190
I'm glad I didn't know that before I jumped in, but honestly, it felt so refreshing and

621
00:25:43.190 --> 00:25:43.810
invigorating.

622
00:25:44.030 --> 00:25:47.010
We all ran back to the sauna and then we did it all over again.

623
00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:50.690
Lady, can I say something about your journal?

624
00:25:51.130 --> 00:25:57.370
It is very clear to me why you love David Sedaris, because your writing reminds me of his writing.

625
00:25:57.470 --> 00:25:58.970
Oh, well, that's a huge compliment.

626
00:25:59.190 --> 00:25:59.530
My goodness.

627
00:25:59.750 --> 00:26:00.190
That's very true.

628
00:26:00.270 --> 00:26:01.110
I never noticed that.

629
00:26:01.110 --> 00:26:02.250
Yes, it's true.

630
00:26:02.550 --> 00:26:06.070
Just all of the little slice of life details.

631
00:26:06.210 --> 00:26:10.730
But then you'll hit me with like watching the sunset with your mom and sisters and like

632
00:26:10.730 --> 00:26:13.650
other details that just like, oh, they just tug at my heartstrings.

633
00:26:13.770 --> 00:26:15.190
It's such a wonderful experience.

634
00:26:15.490 --> 00:26:20.470
And the way it's written, the bless your heart on the tail end of the ponytail thing was like

635
00:26:20.470 --> 00:26:24.490
a perfect David Sedaris level button to like go to a next chapter.

636
00:26:24.670 --> 00:26:28.870
I didn't mean to be snarky to that man, but I just was like, what's the point of that

637
00:26:28.870 --> 00:26:30.870
little rubber band is not holding much?

638
00:26:31.110 --> 00:26:32.710
But he needed it.

639
00:26:32.910 --> 00:26:33.930
He needed that ponytail.

640
00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:34.770
Yes.

641
00:26:35.930 --> 00:26:39.150
So anyway, that was my travel journal.

642
00:26:40.950 --> 00:26:41.390
Wow.

643
00:26:42.710 --> 00:26:43.470
Sorry, Matt.

644
00:26:43.870 --> 00:26:44.870
I loved it.

645
00:26:45.030 --> 00:26:49.290
And also, I'm going to Seattle and I'm definitely going to do that sauna this summer.

646
00:26:49.310 --> 00:26:50.370
Oh, you have to.

647
00:26:50.590 --> 00:26:52.650
I'll share the link in stories, but I'll send it to you.

648
00:26:52.830 --> 00:26:53.690
It was great.

649
00:26:53.690 --> 00:26:54.690
It was not expensive.

650
00:26:54.690 --> 00:26:55.650
I would do it again.

651
00:26:55.890 --> 00:26:56.850
It looked amazing.

652
00:26:57.110 --> 00:26:59.150
Jenna, how much of your journals are about snacks?

653
00:26:59.150 --> 00:27:02.670
I actually have quite a bit of food in my journal.

654
00:27:02.690 --> 00:27:03.130
All right.

655
00:27:03.310 --> 00:27:03.650
Actually.

656
00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:05.050
Oh, I can't wait.

657
00:27:05.190 --> 00:27:07.590
I mean, you guys have never heard me read a travel journal.

658
00:27:07.610 --> 00:27:08.190
No, I haven't.

659
00:27:08.370 --> 00:27:11.650
No, I'm so excited to know this part of you, lady.

660
00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:13.410
I hope you guys like it.

661
00:27:13.650 --> 00:27:16.290
It has a few Angela qualities, I think.

662
00:27:16.530 --> 00:27:17.730
We're not that different.

663
00:27:18.410 --> 00:27:20.070
I don't know what you'll think.

664
00:27:20.290 --> 00:27:21.310
Oh, it's going to be great.

665
00:27:21.310 --> 00:27:22.070
I feel shy.

666
00:27:22.370 --> 00:27:22.570
Well.

667
00:27:22.690 --> 00:27:23.290
Should I start?

668
00:27:23.290 --> 00:27:23.950
Please.

669
00:27:24.370 --> 00:27:26.770
I also titled my journal travel journal.

670
00:27:26.930 --> 00:27:27.210
Okay.

671
00:27:27.890 --> 00:27:34.950
And I said, hello from Tokyo, specifically from the Imperial Hotel in Tokyo, which is the

672
00:27:34.950 --> 00:27:38.130
greatest hotel in the whole world for so many reasons.

673
00:27:38.870 --> 00:27:40.850
First, the location is great.

674
00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:42.550
The beds are firm.

675
00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:43.630
The pillows are soft.

676
00:27:43.830 --> 00:27:49.130
They have these blackout shades that overlap when you close them so there are no gaps.

677
00:27:49.430 --> 00:27:49.710
Ooh.

678
00:27:50.050 --> 00:27:52.150
These are the things that I appreciate in a hotel.

679
00:27:52.150 --> 00:27:54.570
Well, there is a clock in the bathroom.

680
00:27:54.970 --> 00:27:55.390
Thank you.

681
00:27:56.550 --> 00:27:57.350
Also, pajamas.

682
00:27:57.710 --> 00:28:01.370
They lay out pajamas each night, and they have an amazing shower gel.

683
00:28:02.210 --> 00:28:07.130
I was going to ride on the airplane, but instead, I read my book, which I am loving, The Poisonwood

684
00:28:07.130 --> 00:28:11.410
Bible, and I watched The Running Man, which is still kind of haunting me days later.

685
00:28:12.130 --> 00:28:14.410
And once we got here, we hit the ground running.

686
00:28:14.670 --> 00:28:19.130
This is probably our most ambitious travel itinerary, and I'm really impressed with how the kids

687
00:28:19.130 --> 00:28:19.830
have jumped in.

688
00:28:19.830 --> 00:28:23.190
Our first day, we toured around for 11 hours straight.

689
00:28:23.410 --> 00:28:23.910
Wow.

690
00:28:24.730 --> 00:28:27.270
I had a little time this morning, so here we go.

691
00:28:27.710 --> 00:28:33.030
First of all, the thing I'm looking most forward to about this trip is being stuck all together

692
00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:35.550
as a family of four for two solid weeks.

693
00:28:36.030 --> 00:28:39.530
No work, no friends, nothing but the four of us.

694
00:28:39.890 --> 00:28:43.390
I'm also so excited that I don't have to manage our family calendar.

695
00:28:43.390 --> 00:28:48.210
For two weeks, we will all be doing the same thing all together every day.

696
00:28:48.990 --> 00:28:53.630
I'm excited for our actual vacation and also excited that I don't have to figure out how

697
00:28:53.630 --> 00:28:56.130
to get everyone to all their different activities every day.

698
00:28:56.270 --> 00:28:58.910
It's one calendar, one plan.

699
00:28:59.530 --> 00:29:02.210
I think Lee is excited he doesn't have to cook any food.

700
00:29:02.210 --> 00:29:07.150
He does breakfast for everyone each morning, and I'm currently watching him sip coffee and look

701
00:29:07.150 --> 00:29:12.170
out the window at the Tokyo skyline, and I can tell he's so happy he doesn't have to take

702
00:29:12.170 --> 00:29:13.470
anyone's breakfast order.

703
00:29:14.750 --> 00:29:18.230
Also, I am most comfortable in my little family unit.

704
00:29:18.450 --> 00:29:20.210
I don't get social anxiety.

705
00:29:20.490 --> 00:29:21.890
I feel really safe.

706
00:29:22.470 --> 00:29:26.010
Two weeks of immersing in this part of my life is heaven.

707
00:29:26.350 --> 00:29:28.870
Oh, that's so truly you, lady.

708
00:29:28.870 --> 00:29:34.290
Yeah, that was very, um, is it the scientific method that you learn in school where it's like

709
00:29:34.290 --> 00:29:39.290
observation, ask a question, research, form a hypothesis, conduct an experiment,

710
00:29:39.990 --> 00:29:42.330
analyze, draw conclusions, and then communicate results?

711
00:29:42.830 --> 00:29:44.530
That's my journaling style?

712
00:29:44.630 --> 00:29:46.390
Yes, it's perfectly to a T.

713
00:29:46.750 --> 00:29:48.950
This is where I was, this is where I went, this is what I saw.

714
00:29:49.150 --> 00:29:49.850
Well, I go on.

715
00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:55.090
I say, it's only our second day here, and we have already been laughing a lot and making

716
00:29:55.090 --> 00:29:56.950
inside jokes and family memories.

717
00:29:57.250 --> 00:29:58.530
Ack, I just love it.

718
00:29:59.490 --> 00:30:01.670
It was a little brutal our first night.

719
00:30:01.870 --> 00:30:04.570
We didn't sleep on the plane, but we arrived at 7 p.m.

720
00:30:04.650 --> 00:30:06.090
and just had to stay awake for a

721
00:30:06.090 --> 00:30:06.730
few hours.

722
00:30:07.010 --> 00:30:07.730
We did it.

723
00:30:08.070 --> 00:30:10.350
People were a little short-tempered that first day.

724
00:30:10.510 --> 00:30:14.450
We spent a lot of time figuring out how to buy a train ticket to see this giant Buddha.

725
00:30:14.890 --> 00:30:18.390
When you are jet-lagged, it makes machines even harder to understand.

726
00:30:18.970 --> 00:30:23.050
Me, Lee, and Weston were able to use this app on our phone, but it wouldn't let Harper

727
00:30:23.050 --> 00:30:24.630
use it because she's under 13.

728
00:30:24.630 --> 00:30:29.010
So we had to get her a physical ticket or card every time we rode the train.

729
00:30:29.170 --> 00:30:30.030
That was a whole thing.

730
00:30:30.370 --> 00:30:34.910
The train ride to the Buddha was long, and the Buddha was fine, but maybe not worth two

731
00:30:34.910 --> 00:30:36.950
hours there and two hours back on a train.

732
00:30:37.150 --> 00:30:38.210
Sorry, giant Buddha.

733
00:30:38.210 --> 00:30:41.590
We did enjoy the little town and had a pretty cool lunch.

734
00:30:42.250 --> 00:30:45.470
This is something that would also be great on TripAdvisor.

735
00:30:46.190 --> 00:30:48.010
Yeah, this is perfect.

736
00:30:49.690 --> 00:30:54.390
Okay, everyone is in a good mood today after a good night's sleep and a great breakfast

737
00:30:54.390 --> 00:30:54.830
buffet.

738
00:30:55.310 --> 00:30:58.430
I'm not sure it gets better than a breakfast buffet on vacation.

739
00:30:58.910 --> 00:31:03.210
Something that has already surprised me is just how darn good the pineapple is in Japan.

740
00:31:03.530 --> 00:31:09.310
I looked it up, and I read that 90% of Japan's pineapples are grown in Okinawa and that, yes,

741
00:31:09.490 --> 00:31:10.570
they are really sweet.

742
00:31:11.030 --> 00:31:12.510
And I can confirm.

743
00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:16.410
Also, Japanese melon, which I can't quite figure out.

744
00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:19.030
It's sort of like honeydew melon, but not exactly.

745
00:31:19.290 --> 00:31:21.010
It's also really sweet and delicious.

746
00:31:21.770 --> 00:31:26.750
The coffee here is good, but they only served us one small cup at breakfast, and they didn't

747
00:31:26.750 --> 00:31:28.390
come around and ask if we wanted more.

748
00:31:28.610 --> 00:31:33.490
And I think it's because maybe the Japanese don't pound coffee like Americans do, because

749
00:31:33.490 --> 00:31:38.350
I remember being in France, and their whole thing is like, one small great cup of coffee

750
00:31:38.350 --> 00:31:39.350
is all you need.

751
00:31:39.350 --> 00:31:41.450
And I'm like, I beg to differ.

752
00:31:41.710 --> 00:31:44.350
I'm used to three or four decent cups of coffee.

753
00:31:44.710 --> 00:31:48.990
Anyway, this has become a real tangent, but I am fascinated by cultural differences when

754
00:31:48.990 --> 00:31:49.530
I travel.

755
00:31:50.390 --> 00:31:54.310
Oh, like, for example, there are no trash cans in Japan.

756
00:31:54.670 --> 00:31:58.050
They were removed after the subway gas bombing of 1995.

757
00:31:58.590 --> 00:32:03.470
So, if you acquire any trash in your day, you have to carry it around until you get home.

758
00:32:03.850 --> 00:32:06.130
Like, let's say you get a Starbucks.

759
00:32:06.130 --> 00:32:10.750
First of all, no one walks around and eats or walks around and drinks in Japan.

760
00:32:10.970 --> 00:32:15.710
You can get a food or drink item to go, but you have to just stand to the side and eat

761
00:32:15.710 --> 00:32:16.210
or drink.

762
00:32:16.750 --> 00:32:19.410
Also, no eating or drinking on the trains.

763
00:32:19.790 --> 00:32:22.450
On the bullet train, it's okay, but not on the subway.

764
00:32:22.930 --> 00:32:28.210
So, let's say you grab a hot can of coffee from 7-Eleven, because that's another thing.

765
00:32:28.370 --> 00:32:30.930
The 7-Elevens have hot cans of coffee.

766
00:32:31.250 --> 00:32:32.290
There's 7-Elevens?

767
00:32:32.830 --> 00:32:33.570
Oh, yeah.

768
00:32:33.930 --> 00:32:35.090
Famously, 7-Elevens.

769
00:32:35.450 --> 00:32:36.270
Yeah, they're huge there.

770
00:32:36.450 --> 00:32:38.630
And they have all these snacks and sushi.

771
00:32:39.050 --> 00:32:42.150
Yeah, the 7-Eleven, is it egg salad, is supposed to be world-renowned.

772
00:32:42.270 --> 00:32:42.320
Oh, that's right.

773
00:32:42.410 --> 00:32:43.550
I remember hearing this.

774
00:32:43.550 --> 00:32:44.450
You guys, I didn't eat it.

775
00:32:44.590 --> 00:32:45.450
Can you believe it?

776
00:32:45.550 --> 00:32:46.990
Me, lover of eggs.

777
00:32:47.310 --> 00:32:51.350
Every day, my son was like, Mom, are you going to get that egg salad sandwich today?

778
00:32:51.490 --> 00:32:54.750
And every time, I was like, I just don't feel like eating eggs right now.

779
00:32:54.850 --> 00:32:55.790
It's always, like, full.

780
00:32:55.950 --> 00:32:56.130
Yeah.

781
00:33:02.970 --> 00:33:07.170
So, I can't believe I went all the way to Japan, and I didn't eat the egg salad sandwich.

782
00:33:07.470 --> 00:33:11.370
But anyway, okay, back to my journal.

783
00:33:12.170 --> 00:33:17.470
There's a whole section that looks like it's refrigerated and filled with cans, but actually,

784
00:33:17.470 --> 00:33:18.690
they are all hot.

785
00:33:19.010 --> 00:33:21.850
So, let's say you get a hot can of coffee.

786
00:33:22.090 --> 00:33:23.490
You stand and drink it.

787
00:33:23.630 --> 00:33:25.950
And when you're done, zero trash cans.

788
00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:28.070
You have to carry that can around.

789
00:33:28.070 --> 00:33:31.650
You might be thinking, could I throw it away in a public restroom?

790
00:33:31.850 --> 00:33:32.870
Because I thought that.

791
00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:35.270
I thought there might be a trash can in there.

792
00:33:35.530 --> 00:33:36.010
No.

793
00:33:36.430 --> 00:33:40.230
Most public restrooms didn't have paper towels or trash bins.

794
00:33:40.690 --> 00:33:42.030
They had hand dryers.

795
00:33:42.170 --> 00:33:46.170
And if they did have a trash bin, it is not considered polite to throw anything other

796
00:33:46.170 --> 00:33:48.290
than feminine products or paper towels in it.

797
00:33:48.630 --> 00:33:50.950
So, you carry around your trash.

798
00:33:51.110 --> 00:33:53.250
In a bag, in your purse, in your pocket.

799
00:33:53.570 --> 00:33:57.810
I found it sort of fascinating because you really get a sense of how much trash you produce

800
00:33:57.810 --> 00:34:00.130
when you're forced to carry it around with you all day.

801
00:34:01.170 --> 00:34:02.470
And here's the other thing.

802
00:34:02.690 --> 00:34:04.050
The city is spotless.

803
00:34:04.510 --> 00:34:06.310
Everyone picks up after themselves.

804
00:34:06.770 --> 00:34:11.210
I was actually trying to imagine a Japanese person going to New York City and seeing the

805
00:34:11.210 --> 00:34:13.550
city through their eyes, and I shuddered at the thought.

806
00:34:13.730 --> 00:34:16.730
And listen, I love New York, but it's so dirty.

807
00:34:17.550 --> 00:34:18.910
Okay, I should go.

808
00:34:19.090 --> 00:34:20.630
I'll write more later if I'm able.

809
00:34:21.350 --> 00:34:22.110
Ah-ha!

810
00:34:22.410 --> 00:34:23.910
You were talking to your journal.

811
00:34:24.110 --> 00:34:24.710
I was.

812
00:34:24.710 --> 00:34:25.330
It's your friend.

813
00:34:25.630 --> 00:34:26.910
Okay, I gotta go.

814
00:34:26.910 --> 00:34:30.510
I hope you guys realize both of these fit together like puzzle pieces.

815
00:34:30.810 --> 00:34:34.790
If you erase the places that you went, this could be the same story.

816
00:34:35.030 --> 00:34:39.810
Yours is all about how you got there with nothing about what you did when you were there.

817
00:34:39.930 --> 00:34:43.410
I talk about it in hindsight, what I did in places.

818
00:34:44.230 --> 00:34:46.090
True, but not to the extent of Jenna's.

819
00:34:46.210 --> 00:34:46.630
No, no.

820
00:34:46.810 --> 00:34:48.330
You didn't mention once how everyone's moods were.

821
00:34:48.550 --> 00:34:51.470
No, I didn't talk about other people.

822
00:34:51.690 --> 00:34:53.930
I didn't talk about my family dynamics.

823
00:34:53.930 --> 00:34:56.350
You guys see that you fill in each other's stories like that.

824
00:34:56.490 --> 00:34:57.970
Maybe this is why we're BFFs.

825
00:34:57.970 --> 00:34:58.470
I know.

826
00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:02.890
This is why our points of view meld so well together into a podcaSt.

827
00:35:03.110 --> 00:35:04.370
We compliment each other.

828
00:35:04.510 --> 00:35:04.990
We do.

829
00:35:05.290 --> 00:35:05.340
All.

830
00:35:05.570 --> 00:35:06.510
I have more.

831
00:35:06.890 --> 00:35:07.330
Please.

832
00:35:07.590 --> 00:35:07.850
Okay.

833
00:35:07.990 --> 00:35:09.890
I want you to read the entire thing.

834
00:35:10.070 --> 00:35:11.230
Okay, here's the rest of it.

835
00:35:11.310 --> 00:35:11.450
Ready?

836
00:35:11.450 --> 00:35:12.990
We love Tokyo.

837
00:35:13.570 --> 00:35:17.210
We head to Kyoto tomorrow and we kind of wish we had another day here.

838
00:35:17.810 --> 00:35:22.230
Lee and I came years ago, just the two of us, and we liked it, but visiting it again,

839
00:35:22.390 --> 00:35:23.350
we've fallen in love.

840
00:35:23.770 --> 00:35:27.250
One of the things we are discovering is how vertical the city is.

841
00:35:27.490 --> 00:35:29.490
You have to get off the ground level.

842
00:35:29.770 --> 00:35:32.050
The best stores and restaurants are up.

843
00:35:32.550 --> 00:35:37.510
The other night, we ate at a Wagyu barbecue place near Ueno Park, and one of the best parts

844
00:35:37.510 --> 00:35:40.150
of the meal was riding the elevator to the 11th floor.

845
00:35:40.150 --> 00:35:45.270
We stopped at a bunch of floors along the way, and every time the doors opened, they opened

846
00:35:45.270 --> 00:35:46.630
into a different restaurant.

847
00:35:46.750 --> 00:35:47.510
Oh, cool.

848
00:35:47.810 --> 00:35:53.350
And you would get this tiny glimpse into a whole new world, a whole vibe with new smells

849
00:35:53.350 --> 00:35:57.230
and sounds, and then the doors would shut and take you up to the next spot.

850
00:35:57.550 --> 00:36:00.010
I wanted to stop at every floor.

851
00:36:00.510 --> 00:36:02.950
One time, the doors opened on a giant fish tank.

852
00:36:03.110 --> 00:36:04.830
That's all, just a fish tank.

853
00:36:05.010 --> 00:36:09.430
We are still all talking about it days later, and I keep thinking about it.

854
00:36:09.430 --> 00:36:14.770
It was like riding in a real-life choose-your-own-adventure book, each stop having the potential to

855
00:36:14.770 --> 00:36:15.770
change your destiny.

856
00:36:17.090 --> 00:36:21.310
Another highlight of the trip was going to the Tokyo Dome to see the Tokyo Giants play

857
00:36:21.310 --> 00:36:21.830
the Tigers.

858
00:36:22.130 --> 00:36:26.450
I don't know where the Tigers were from, but I know it was the Giants versus the Tigers,

859
00:36:26.510 --> 00:36:27.650
and this is a big rivalry.

860
00:36:28.230 --> 00:36:33.130
We got to the game and bought some Giants hats, and we're all so excited, and then we realized

861
00:36:33.130 --> 00:36:34.890
we were sitting in the Tigers section.

862
00:36:35.150 --> 00:36:35.370
Oh, no!

863
00:36:36.050 --> 00:36:40.930
So it was kind of hilarious, because they have dedicated sections for fans, and they have

864
00:36:40.930 --> 00:36:42.890
all these chants they do during the game.

865
00:36:43.070 --> 00:36:46.430
It reminded me of the chants at the LAFC soccer games.

866
00:36:46.670 --> 00:36:48.750
Just constant chanting, but so fun.

867
00:36:49.330 --> 00:36:51.870
There is a small theme park surrounding the Dome.

868
00:36:51.990 --> 00:36:57.150
It is smaller than Six Flags or Disneyland, but they do have a log flume ride and one of

869
00:36:57.150 --> 00:37:02.590
those big swinging pirate ships, and it has one of the tallest, continuous roller coasters

870
00:37:02.590 --> 00:37:04.870
in the world, currently ranked number 10.

871
00:37:05.670 --> 00:37:06.950
Lee went on it with Harper.

872
00:37:07.470 --> 00:37:08.930
Ooh, they loved it.

873
00:37:09.110 --> 00:37:11.270
They said the initial drop was insane.

874
00:37:11.670 --> 00:37:14.610
And of course, there was a place with a gazillion crane machines.

875
00:37:14.750 --> 00:37:18.030
We spent a lot of yen trying to win a plushie, but went home empty-handed.

876
00:37:18.570 --> 00:37:22.230
Maybe the coolest thing we did in Tokyo was our Shibuya food tour.

877
00:37:22.550 --> 00:37:26.930
We had a local guide take us through the underground food stalls of the food show

878
00:37:26.930 --> 00:37:28.730
beneath Shibuya train station.

879
00:37:29.230 --> 00:37:34.130
He explained that all of the food stalls were pop-up versions of some of the best restaurants

880
00:37:34.130 --> 00:37:35.790
from the best chefs in Tokyo.

881
00:37:36.350 --> 00:37:40.690
There was a place selling bento boxes made by a three-star Michelin chef.

882
00:37:41.170 --> 00:37:46.410
Our first stop was a six-stool ramen place that had been voted best chicken ramen in Tokyo

883
00:37:46.410 --> 00:37:47.950
for several years in a row.

884
00:37:48.070 --> 00:37:49.450
You're making me so hungry.

885
00:37:49.710 --> 00:37:52.490
Also, you're describing a place I've always wanted to go to.

886
00:37:52.570 --> 00:37:54.510
I know exactly what you're referencing right now.

887
00:37:54.510 --> 00:37:57.670
Um, Sam, it was amazing.

888
00:37:58.230 --> 00:38:00.130
That's the next line in my journal.

889
00:38:00.330 --> 00:38:01.690
And it was amazing.

890
00:38:02.050 --> 00:38:03.550
Sam, it was amazing.

891
00:38:03.810 --> 00:38:04.630
Yeah, yeah.

892
00:38:05.890 --> 00:38:08.450
They served the ramen with a slice of lemon,

893
00:38:08.450 --> 00:38:12.210
and we were told to squeeze the lemon into the bowl about halfway through

894
00:38:12.210 --> 00:38:15.170
as a way to change the flavor complexity of the broth.

895
00:38:16.150 --> 00:38:20.370
Afterward, our guide walked us through the supermarket attached to the food stalls.

896
00:38:20.370 --> 00:38:24.330
He showed us these fancy cantaloupes that cost around $70 or $80.

897
00:38:24.970 --> 00:38:25.510
Whoa!

898
00:38:25.990 --> 00:38:28.450
He said the Japanese give fruit as a gift,

899
00:38:28.470 --> 00:38:31.510
and the nicer-looking the fruit, the higher the price,

900
00:38:31.650 --> 00:38:33.290
even though they taste the same.

901
00:38:33.730 --> 00:38:36.770
With the cantaloupe, the deeper the grooves, the more expensive.

902
00:38:37.270 --> 00:38:41.650
He also said if someone is in the hospital, you give fruit or cut flowers,

903
00:38:41.730 --> 00:38:44.690
but never planted flowers or anything with roots,

904
00:38:44.690 --> 00:38:48.150
because that implies you want them to put down roots in their illness.

905
00:38:50.030 --> 00:38:52.910
We ended with a trip to a teppanyaki restaurant.

906
00:38:53.230 --> 00:38:54.990
We were the only tourist there.

907
00:38:55.250 --> 00:38:57.050
No one spoke English, no English menu.

908
00:38:57.250 --> 00:38:59.010
So it's a good thing we were with him.

909
00:38:59.310 --> 00:39:02.410
I feel like we would have been too intimidated to go to this restaurant otherwise.

910
00:39:02.850 --> 00:39:06.050
He ordered all the food, and it was seriously all amazing.

911
00:39:06.410 --> 00:39:08.450
My favorite were the meat-stuffed green peppers,

912
00:39:08.690 --> 00:39:10.530
the fried ginger root, and the chicken thighs.

913
00:39:11.230 --> 00:39:13.350
We are so sad to leave Tokyo.

914
00:39:13.750 --> 00:39:17.430
We wanted to ride more elevators and see what the doors opened onto.

915
00:39:17.430 --> 00:39:21.510
I really wish I could have explored more of the Shibuya and the food market.

916
00:39:21.750 --> 00:39:24.910
I wanted to buy one of those bento boxes to eat on the train tomorrow,

917
00:39:25.070 --> 00:39:27.690
but they made it very clear you had to eat it the same day.

918
00:39:28.570 --> 00:39:29.610
Maybe one day.

919
00:39:30.150 --> 00:39:32.410
I am excited to get out into nature a bit,

920
00:39:32.450 --> 00:39:34.290
and we have fun stuff planned for Kyoto.

921
00:39:34.430 --> 00:39:36.790
We are going to make pottery with a local artist,

922
00:39:36.970 --> 00:39:39.970
and then he fires the pieces and sends them back home to us.

923
00:39:40.110 --> 00:39:40.930
I can't wait.

924
00:39:41.310 --> 00:39:43.230
This has already been an incredible trip,

925
00:39:43.410 --> 00:39:44.950
and we are making great memories.

926
00:39:45.510 --> 00:39:47.330
Oh, I love that, lady.

927
00:39:47.990 --> 00:39:49.730
And guys, I have to tell you,

928
00:39:49.930 --> 00:39:52.090
when it was time for us to fly home,

929
00:39:52.330 --> 00:39:55.150
our flight was delayed by five hours,

930
00:39:55.150 --> 00:39:58.450
so we got to spend extra time in Tokyo.

931
00:39:58.690 --> 00:40:01.370
We went back to the Shibuya food show,

932
00:40:01.450 --> 00:40:03.650
and I got that bento box.

933
00:40:03.670 --> 00:40:03.950
Good.

934
00:40:04.090 --> 00:40:06.450
And it was so good.

935
00:40:06.450 --> 00:40:06.750
Yeah.

936
00:40:06.750 --> 00:40:07.710
And they're not expensive.

937
00:40:07.710 --> 00:40:10.550
That's part of the thing about these food stalls.

938
00:40:10.550 --> 00:40:13.250
That was one of Anthony Bourdain's favorite spots.

939
00:40:13.630 --> 00:40:13.950
Really?

940
00:40:13.990 --> 00:40:14.830
That's why I know about it.

941
00:40:14.910 --> 00:40:16.050
Yeah, he raves about it.

942
00:40:16.130 --> 00:40:19.030
I mean, I never wanted to leave.

943
00:40:19.270 --> 00:40:20.890
They also had this crazy thing.

944
00:40:21.450 --> 00:40:22.750
They have lockers,

945
00:40:22.770 --> 00:40:26.030
so if in the morning you want to go shopping and buy stuff,

946
00:40:26.030 --> 00:40:27.470
you can put it in the locker,

947
00:40:27.510 --> 00:40:28.890
and they're refrigerated.

948
00:40:29.110 --> 00:40:29.570
Oh, nice.

949
00:40:29.570 --> 00:40:31.710
They have refrigerated lockers,

950
00:40:31.710 --> 00:40:35.730
and they have a little section where you can warm stuff up in a microwave and eat it.

951
00:40:35.870 --> 00:40:36.590
That's incredible.

952
00:40:36.790 --> 00:40:37.070
Amazing.

953
00:40:37.070 --> 00:40:38.050
So smart.

954
00:40:38.730 --> 00:40:40.330
Well, Jenna, I loved your journal,

955
00:40:40.330 --> 00:40:43.470
and it's like a combination of like a trip advisor

956
00:40:43.470 --> 00:40:46.650
and like just wonderful specific details,

957
00:40:46.890 --> 00:40:51.450
but then just hearing your heart being so happy with your family.

958
00:40:51.650 --> 00:40:52.090
I loved it.

959
00:40:52.630 --> 00:40:53.450
Aw, thank you.

960
00:40:53.510 --> 00:40:54.870
Well, I love your journaling.

961
00:40:55.030 --> 00:40:56.310
I truly love it.

962
00:40:57.690 --> 00:41:01.410
And not that we need to justify the importance of journals,

963
00:41:01.930 --> 00:41:03.670
but we did get two letters,

964
00:41:04.270 --> 00:41:05.930
and I want to share them with you, Angela,

965
00:41:05.930 --> 00:41:07.610
because they really touched my heart,

966
00:41:07.750 --> 00:41:09.530
and they are about your journaling.

967
00:41:10.550 --> 00:41:11.230
Kaylee W.

968
00:41:11.590 --> 00:41:13.650
from Sarasota Springs, New York, said,

969
00:41:13.990 --> 00:41:18.390
I started listening to your podcast in 2022 when my daughter was born,

970
00:41:18.430 --> 00:41:20.710
and I was up all night breastfeeding and pumping.

971
00:41:21.190 --> 00:41:23.790
You ladies kept me company through the long nights,

972
00:41:23.950 --> 00:41:25.610
and I am forever grateful for that.

973
00:41:26.290 --> 00:41:28.590
Fast forward, my daughter's almost three,

974
00:41:28.850 --> 00:41:31.570
and she's had a tough time falling asleep lately,

975
00:41:31.570 --> 00:41:34.730
so I have been popping in an AirPod and listening to old episodes.

976
00:41:35.490 --> 00:41:38.890
Last night, I was listening to episode 139, Christening,

977
00:41:39.070 --> 00:41:40.910
and I knew I had to write in.

978
00:41:41.570 --> 00:41:44.630
I was laying in bed trying to be so quiet,

979
00:41:44.910 --> 00:41:46.290
waiting for my daughter to settle

980
00:41:46.290 --> 00:41:48.690
when Angela started reading her journal entries.

981
00:41:49.570 --> 00:41:53.230
I burst into laughter and had to get it under control so fast,

982
00:41:53.290 --> 00:41:55.430
I legitimately felt like I was going to cry

983
00:41:55.430 --> 00:41:57.070
from trying to stifle my laugh.

984
00:41:57.070 --> 00:41:59.930
And I wanted to write you both for a couple of reasons.

985
00:42:00.250 --> 00:42:02.850
My husband and I recently started talking about

986
00:42:02.850 --> 00:42:06.830
how we find our daughter so unique, funny, and passionate,

987
00:42:06.850 --> 00:42:10.070
and we don't want her to ever lose that sense of herself.

988
00:42:10.270 --> 00:42:13.030
But no, it often happens when kids start school.

989
00:42:14.130 --> 00:42:18.090
Personally, I can never even reread my old journal entries

990
00:42:18.090 --> 00:42:19.910
because I feel too embarrassed.

991
00:42:20.770 --> 00:42:24.290
Hearing you do it on such a public platform,

992
00:42:25.030 --> 00:42:28.230
this choked me up, Angela, because this is true.

993
00:42:28.970 --> 00:42:32.530
Kaylee said, hearing you do it on such a public platform

994
00:42:32.530 --> 00:42:36.110
made me stop and think about how I hope my daughter

995
00:42:36.110 --> 00:42:38.170
is that confident in herself,

996
00:42:38.170 --> 00:42:42.050
and I am going to do my best to instill that in her.

997
00:42:43.610 --> 00:42:45.210
Because it's true.

998
00:42:45.990 --> 00:42:50.610
That is you giving us your whole self,

999
00:42:50.610 --> 00:42:52.190
and that takes a lot of courage,

1000
00:42:52.190 --> 00:42:54.290
and it takes a lot of confidence.

1001
00:42:54.290 --> 00:42:56.570
And I love that Kaylee pointed that out.

1002
00:42:56.710 --> 00:42:58.450
Oh, Kaylee, thank you so much.

1003
00:42:58.770 --> 00:43:00.050
All right, here's one more.

1004
00:43:00.850 --> 00:43:01.410
Andy D.

1005
00:43:01.690 --> 00:43:02.630
from Georgia said,

1006
00:43:03.070 --> 00:43:03.570
Hello.

1007
00:43:04.090 --> 00:43:06.990
I want to say I know Angela gets a lot of grief

1008
00:43:06.990 --> 00:43:08.150
for her journal entries,

1009
00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:12.810
but those kind of documents are things that historians love

1010
00:43:12.810 --> 00:43:16.150
because they document in detail normal life activities

1011
00:43:16.150 --> 00:43:17.990
that are difficult to get

1012
00:43:17.990 --> 00:43:20.310
just from general historical records.

1013
00:43:21.370 --> 00:43:23.910
If, when, the zombie apocalypse

1014
